  "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to convert $12^5$ from base 10 to base $b$. Then, we need to find the largest base $b$ such that the sum of the digits of this base-$b$ number does not equal 41. We will go through the steps systematically.\n\n### Step 1: Convert \\(12^5\\) from base 10 to various bases\n\nFirst, we convert \\(12^5\\) from base 10 to different bases and look at the sum of the digits in each base.\n\n### Step 2: Convert \\(12^5\\)\n\\[\n12^5 = 248832\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Check different bases\nWe check various bases such as 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, and 10, \\(123\\), and \\(456\\).\n\n### Step 4: Check base 2\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 2^7 = 12^5 \\times 128 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 2 representation of \\(12^5\\) is \\(12_5 = 1010000_2\\).\nSum of digits = \\(1+0+1+0+0+0+0 = 2\\).\nNot equal to 41, so base 2 works.\n\n### Step 5: Check base 3\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 3^5 = 12^5 \\times 3^5 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 3 representation of \\(12^5\\) is \\(123\\).\nSum of digits = \\(1+2+3 = 6\\).\nNot equal to 41, so base 3 works.\n\n### Step 6: Check base 4\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 4^7 = 12^5 \\times 4^7 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 4 representation of \\(12^5\\) is \\(123\\).\nSum of digits = \\(1+2+3 = 6\\).\nNot equal to 41, so base 4 works.\n\n### Step 7: Check base 5\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 5^7 = 12^5 \\times 5^7 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 5 representation of \\(12^5\\) is \\(12\\).\nSum of digits = \\(1+2 = 3\\).\nNot equal to 41, so base 5 works.\n\n### Step 8: Check base 6\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 6^7 = 12^5 \\times 6^7 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 6 representation of \\(12^5\\) is $123.$\nSum of digits = $1+2+3 = 6$.\nNot equal to 41, so base 6 works.\n\n### Step 9: Check base 7\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 7^7 = 12^5 \\times 7^7 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 7 representation of \\(12^5\\) is $123_{(7)}$.\nSum of digits = $1+2+3 = 6$.\nNot equal to 41, so base 7 works.\n\n### Step 10: Check base 8\n\\[\n12^5 \\times 8^7 = 12^5 \\times 8^7 = 12^7\n\\]\nThe base 8 representation of \\(12^5\\) is $123$.\nSum of digits = $1+2+3 = 6$.\nNot equal to 41, so base 8 works.\n\n### Step 11: Check base 9 and 10\nFor these bases, \\(12^5\\) is expressed as \\(12_{(9)}\\) and \\(12\\_{ (10)}\\).\nSo,\n\\[\n\\text{base 9: } \\sum \\text{digits} = 1 + 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{not equal to 41}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{base 10: } \\sum \\text{digits} = 1 + 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{not equal to 41}\n\\]\n\n### Step 12: Check mass bases\n\\[\n12^5 \\times \\text{Numbers of certain size}^- \\text{i.e. no sum of } 41 for base 5 and big bases: sum \\text{of digits} < 41.\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nLargest base working up to required criteria:\n\\[\nb = 8.\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
